    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense from a CNS standpoint. I'm curious though, when you say \"shock the system into adaptation,\" is there a specific way to time that? Like, should I be looking at metabolic stress or just changes in the rep ranges to make sure the plateau actually breaks?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To truly shock the system, you'll want to oscillate between periods of high mechanical tension and high metabolic stress. Instead of just changing rep ranges, try implementing a block where you vary the load significantly—perhaps moving from heavy, low-rep work to higher-rep, moderate-load work. This constant variation forces different adaptive pathways to engage, preventing the body from becoming accustomed to a single stimulus, which is key to overcoming plateaus.",
      "source": "target",
