    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Got it, so it's about forcing those different adaptation pathways. I'm just trying to figure out if the plateau feels like it's caused by a physical limit or if it's just like... some weird, unexplained stagnation that makes no sense given their nutrition and sleep. Sometimes it feels like training against something stubborn that isn't even there. Any specific signs that it's more of a systemic issue rather than just needing more weight?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "If nutrition and sleep appear optimized, subtle indicators of systemic fatigue might include persistent joint aches, altered recovery times between sets, or a drop in overall mood or motivation. These sensations often signal central nervous system fatigue rather than just muscular lagging. If the squat feels like a stubborn resistance despite good recovery markers, prioritizing a full deload or briefly changing the movement pattern (like switching from back squat to safety bar squat) can often uncover the physical bottleneck.",
      "source": "target",
